---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedGlossaryLinks from '../../../components/RelatedGlossaryLinks.astro';
import RelatedRecordLinks from '../../../components/RelatedRecordLinks.astro';

const layers = [
  {
    title: 'Marketplace frontend',
    copy: 'The website or app surface people use to search collections, create listings, view pages, and navigate marketplace-specific account features.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Marketplace operation',
    copy: 'The business or product operation behind the marketplace. It may end, merge, rebrand, or become limited while other technical traces still exist.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Smart contracts',
    copy: 'On-chain contracts may remain readable even after a marketplace frontend closes, depending on chain status, contract design, and available explorers or tools.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Metadata and media',
    copy: 'NFT metadata and media can depend on storage paths. A marketplace closing does not automatically prove metadata or media disappeared.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Wallet visibility',
    copy: 'Wallets or external tools may still display tokens if they can read the token and metadata paths.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Archives and sources',
    copy: 'Archived pages, public notices, docs, reports, and other traces can remain useful even when the original site changes or vanishes.'
  }
];

const recordQuestions = [
  'Is the original marketplace frontend still reachable?',
  'Did the marketplace operation end, merge, rebrand, or become limited?',
  'Are contracts, metadata, or asset display paths separately documented?',
  'Is there an archived page or source-backed notice?',
  'Is the status clear, or should the record keep uncertainty visible?'
];

const glossaryLinks = [
  { title: 'Marketplace frontend', href: '/glossary/marketplace-frontend/' },
  { title: 'Smart contract', href: '/glossary/smart-contract/' },
  { title: 'Asset metadata', href: '/glossary/asset-metadata/' },
  { title: 'Contract deprecated', href: '/glossary/contract-deprecated/' }
];

const relatedRecords = [
  { title: 'Sequence Marketplace', href: '/encyclopedia/sequence-marketplace/', note: 'Infrastructure-style marketplace record useful for comparing frontend, tooling, and contract review boundaries.' },
  { title: 'RECUR Marketplace', href: '/encyclopedia/recur-marketplace/', note: 'Inactive / under-review record with separate frontend, contract, and asset-status review flags.' },
  { title: 'Mythical Market', href: '/encyclopedia/mythical-market/', note: 'Gaming asset marketplace example where public surface, ecosystem, and asset paths may need separate review.' }
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Frontend vs smart contract: what actually remains? — Minted & Gone" description="A field-guide explanation of frontend closure, marketplace operation, smart contracts, metadata, wallets, and archives as separate layers in NFT marketplace history.">
  <div class="shell guide-page">
    <section class="field-hero simple-hero compact-hero">
      <div class="hero-botanical" aria-hidden="true">✦</div>
      <div class="hero-copy plate-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
        <h1>Frontend vs smart contract: what actually remains?</h1>
        <div class="flourish" aria-hidden="true">✦</div>
        <p class="lede">A closed marketplace website is not the same thing as a vanished on-chain record.</p>
        <p>This guide separates the public frontend, marketplace operation, smart contracts, metadata, wallet visibility, third-party views, and archived source trail.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="/encyclopedia/">Browse records</a><a class="btn" href="/guides/">Back to guides</a></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <p class="mock-note">This guide is educational. It does not verify any specific token, wallet, contract, recovery path, ownership claim, or marketplace safety claim.</p>

    <section class="framed-panel definition-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Short answer</p>
      <h2>Different layers can fail, remain, or become uncertain at different times</h2>
      <p>An NFT marketplace can lose its visible frontend while some on-chain or archived traces remain. A smart contract can remain readable while the original product is no longer operated. Metadata may remain available, become partly unavailable, or need further review.</p>
      <p>Minted &amp; Gone records these layers separately so a closed frontend is not automatically treated as proof that assets, contracts, metadata, or history disappeared.</p>
    </section>

    <RelatedGlossaryLinks terms={glossaryLinks} />
    <RelatedRecordLinks records={relatedRecords} />

    <section class="guide-grid numbered">
      {layers.map((layer, index) => (
        <article class="framed-panel guide-card">
          <span class="num">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <h2>{layer.title}</h2>
          <p>{layer.copy}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section class="framed-panel definition-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why this distinction matters</p>
      <h2>Marketplace history is not a single switch</h2>
      <p>Many NFT marketplace stories are uneven. The domain might redirect. The app might stop loading. Collection pages might disappear. A company might keep another product alive. Contracts might still be readable. External marketplaces or wallets might display some assets. Archives might preserve the old interface.</p>
      <p>Because these layers change separately, Minted &amp; Gone avoids broad claims like "the NFT disappeared" unless a source-backed record supports a narrower statement.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="framed-panel definition-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">How M&amp;G records it</p>
      <h2>Records ask layer-specific questions</h2>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        {recordQuestions.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p>When the answer is unclear, the record should keep the uncertainty visible through notes, evidence, confidence, archive state, and review flags.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-grid">
      <article class="framed-panel guide-card">
        <h2>Frontend closed</h2>
        <p>This usually means the public marketplace interface is no longer available in its original form. It does not by itself settle contract, metadata, or wallet visibility.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="framed-panel guide-card">
        <h2>Contract readable</h2>
        <p>This means an on-chain artifact may still be inspectable. It does not prove that the original marketplace is still operating or that every asset display path works.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="framed-panel guide-card">
        <h2>Archive available</h2>
        <p>This means a historical copy or archive search exists. It is evidence for history, not proof that the marketplace is currently active.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="framed-panel definition-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Related reading</p>
      <h2>Use this guide with individual records</h2>
      <p>Open a marketplace record and compare the status, timeline events, evidence notes, archive URL, and what-remains block. The strongest records are not necessarily the simplest; they are the ones that keep the evidence and uncertainty visible.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="/encyclopedia/">Browse marketplace records</a><a class="btn" href="/guides/what-happens-when-nft-marketplace-shuts-down/">Previous guide</a><a class="btn" href="/methodology/">Read methodology</a></div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>